UPDATE companies SET description='West Hartford Solar operates from West Hartford, Connecticut. The company was founded by Dan Hovey, who serves as owner and principal consultant, with a mission to make solar energy accessible, personal, and ethical. What began as a solo endeavor has expanded into a regional team serving customers across New England.

The firm functions as a licensed solar installer managing projects from design and permitting through installation and activation. It maintains installation crews in Connecticut and supplements coverage in Massachusetts through a partnership with Earthlight Technologies and in New York through New York State Solar Farm. The team includes senior consultants covering western Massachusetts, the Connecticut River Valley, southeastern Connecticut, and the northwestern hills.

Customers range from homeowners seeking to reduce energy costs to businesses, schools, nonprofits, and municipalities pursuing long-term sustainability goals. The company coordinates custom solar solutions for corporate facilities, housing authorities, and local government projects across Connecticut, Massachusetts, and New York.

Hovey started the business to provide transparent pricing, personalized guidance, and a stress-free experience, emphasizing honesty and responsiveness at every step. The company highlights access to multiple financing options and a commitment to tailoring each system to the specific property rather than applying a one-size-fits-all approach.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Solar system design", "Permitting", "Solar panel installation", "System activation", "Project coordination", "Financing options consultation"], "industries": ["Residential", "Commercial", "Municipal", "Education", "Nonprofit"], "materials": [], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Transparent pricing with no hidden markups", "Personal guidance from a local rep backed by a regional team", "In-house management of design, permitting, installation, and activation", "Partnerships with Earthlight Technologies and New York State Solar Farm for multi-state coverage", "Customized system design for each property"], "facts_used": ["Founded by Dan Hovey -> /", "Headquartered in West Hartford, Connecticut -> /", "Licensed solar installer serving CT, MA, NY -> /", "Install crews in Connecticut; partners with Earthlight Technologies (MA) and New York State Solar Farm (NY) -> /", "Team includes senior consultants for multiple regions -> /", "Serves homeowners, businesses, schools, nonprofits, municipalities -> /", "Manages design, permitting, installation, activation in-house -> /", "Mission: make solar accessible, personal, ethical -> /"], "company_type": "Installer / service company", "makes_products": "no", "type_evidence": "West Hartford Solar is a licensed solar installer serving Connecticut, Massachusetts, and New York. We manage every step of your project in house, from design and permitting through installation and activation", "founded_year": "", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='west-hartford-solar' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='The project originated in 2009 when a $62.5-million allocation from the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act was awarded to the Tennessee Department of Economic and Community Development. The University of Tennessee Research Foundation was contracted to oversee creation and operation of the facility, which was designed and built by Signal Energy, LLC of Chattanooga in 2011, with grid connection achieved in early 2012.

The farm comprises approximately 21,000 fixed-tilt, ground-mounted photovoltaic panels generating electricity sold under purchase agreements to Chickasaw Electric Cooperative and the Tennessee Valley Authority. Alongside power production, the SPECTRUM exhibit provides hands-on learning stations, games, and QR-linked resources to teach solar energy basics to students, homeowners, and business visitors. The exhibit partners include Oak Ridge National Laboratory, the Tennessee Valley Authority, and multiple state agencies.

The facility serves utilities, educational institutions, and the general public. Its electricity feeds the regional grid through TVA and Chickasaw Electric Cooperative, while the exhibit attracts school groups, science-fair participants, and homeowners exploring rooftop solar. Partners such as the U.S. Department of Energy, Tennessee Advanced Energy Business Council, and Volkswagen reflect a broad stakeholder base spanning government, research, and industry.

The project was conceived as a dual-purpose asset: a revenue-generating solar array and a public showcase for renewable energy technology. Its backers emphasized job creation, education, and technology commercialization as core goals, positioning the site as a living laboratory for solar adoption in Tennessee.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Utility-scale solar power generation", "Interactive solar energy education", "Public outreach and STEM education"], "industries": ["Advanced energy", "Solar power", "Renewable energy", "Education"], "materials": ["Photovoltaic solar panels"], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Utility-scale solar generation paired with interactive public exhibit", "Funded by $62.5M ARRA allocation in 2009", "Electricity sold to TVA and Chickasaw Electric Cooperative under purchase agreements", "Designed and built by Signal Energy, LLC in 2011"], "facts_used": ["Location: Stanton, Tennessee -> /", "Founding year: 2009 (ARRA allocation) -> /", "Ownership: University of Tennessee Research Foundation -> /", "Facility: 25 acres, ~21,000 fixed-tilt ground-mounted PV panels -> /", "Builder: Signal Energy, LLC (2011) -> /", "Grid connection: early 2012 -> /", "Off-takers: Chickasaw Electric Cooperative and Tennessee Valley Authority -> /", "Exhibit name: SPECTRUM -> /", "Exhibit partners: Oak Ridge National Laboratory, TVA, state agencies, universities -> /", "Purpose: job creation, education, renewable power production, technology commercialization -> /"], "company_type": "Other", "makes_products": "no", "type_evidence": "More than 25 acres contain a solar array of approximately 21,000 fixed-tilt, ground-mounted photovoltaic solar panels. Electricity generated is distributed through purchase agreements with local utility Chickasaw Electric Cooperative and Tennessee Valley Authority.", "founded_year": "2009", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='west-tennessee-solar-exhibit' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='West Virginia Steel Corporation operates from Poca, West Virginia, as a steel supplier and fabricator serving industrial, commercial, and mining markets. The company is part of the RMIS Corporation group, which traces its roots to 1978, though West Virginia Steel’s own founding date is not publicly stated. Ownership details such as family or veteran status are not disclosed.

The company maintains one of West Virginia’s largest steel inventories and offers fabrication up to 2,000 tons, including CNC plasma cutting of plate up to six inches thick. All structural work follows AISC guidelines, covering components for material handling systems, cleaning systems, preparation facilities, heavy construction equipment, and specialty buildings. Turn-key steel packages have been delivered for prep plants across Appalachia, the Midwest, and internationally.

Customers span medical, educational, religious, manufacturing, and commercial construction, with notable projects including the Laidley Towers in Charleston and modules for U.S. naval battleships. The firm also supports mining operations with underground services, electrical installations, and temporary skilled labor.

